WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.675
What questions should a school ask
families if it wants to improve how it

00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:04.250
communicates?

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.370
Think of a parent survey like a
flashlight.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:11.150
Aim it at one decision and you can see
what’s getting in the way.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.390
Point it everywhere and you may still
leave with guesses.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.990
Imagine a school that wants more families
to use its after-school program.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.570
Ask whether they saw the invitation first.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.150
Then ask what made signing up harder.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.690
A late reminder and an inconvenient pickup
time are two different problems.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:34.175
The Kansas State Department of Education’s
family engagement survey is one example

00:00:34.175 --> 00:00:38.190
of asking about specific experiences,
including school communication.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.750
Test your own questions with families
before launch.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.830
Offer more than one way to respond and
check whose voices you missed.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.210
Tell families what you heard and what
you’ll try next.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:55.090
If your district needs help planning the
research, visit prcimpact.com

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.535
or call PRC Impact at

00:00:57.535 --> 00:01:01.150
(334) 429-6994.
